clean_summary:
If you're willing to put up with explicit communications in your code, this is our latest direction for addressing some of the silent performance cliffs in DTensor. There's tradeoff space here for explicit-implicit, and spmd_types stakes out a different spot on the curve.

@kamilsindi: "Distributed training is hard. We adopted DTensor at Runway to prevent silent gradient bugs and it delivered. But we traded performance for correctness, hitting dispatch overhead, recompilation storms, and MFU drops. Wrote up what we learned and how we work around it."

The linked GitHub module defines a type system for distributed training code, based on JAX's sharding in types, but adapted for the PyTorch ecosystem.

This is not just nightlies, it is in 2.12! Thanks to Natalia for the correction.

@ezyang: "Pro-tip: using CUDA graphs and annoyed that all the kernels have no labels in your profiles? Get a nightly that has mark_kernels context manager. You need the 13.1 driver, but user mode driver is enough."

The linked pull request adds a kernel annotation module for CUDA graph traces, enabling better profiling with labeled kernels in GPU-accelerated dynamic neural networks.

I've let Fable optimize GPU kernels autonomously using the auto-gpu-kernel harness. If it joined NVIDIA's competition today, it would have won in 4 out of 5 kernels against humans.

Fable can write Gluon kernels, perform warp-specialization, and use TMA tcgen05, achieving speedups versus Opus 4.8.

@dogacel0 is testing Mythos for GPU kernel generation on three kernels: DSA, GDN, and MoE routing, comparing its performance to Opus 4.7, which previously beat humans in the DSA track.

